SUMMARY:
The manuscript is substantially accurate, carefully attributed, and well-constructed. Every material factual claim traces to the witnessed Grist source. The prose is specific, avoids the common failure modes of this beat, and respects the individuality of the named First Nations communities. The piece is not publishable in its current form for one bounded and concrete reason: every inline citation in the body uses a non-canonical URL case variant (`/Indigenous/` with a capital I) rather than the admitted canonical path (`/indigenous/`). A correction record in the frontmatter claims this defect was resolved, but the body text was not updated to match. That inconsistency â€” a correction logged but not applied â€” is the singular blocker. A targeted repair to the five affected hyperlinks, followed by gate clearance, would make the piece publishable.

FINDINGS:
The piece does what it sets out to do. It opens on Namaygoosisagagun (Collins) First Nation as a specific, concrete anchor and expands accurately into the wider Ontario and national wildfire picture before returning to the recognition-gap legal argument that gives the story its structural spine. The headline and lede are faithful to the sourced facts; neither strengthens an allegation into a proven claim nor compresses the scope of the crisis into something larger than the evidence supports. The prose treats named Nations as distinct entities rather than collapsing them into a single "Indigenous communities" abstraction, which is the correct handling for this subject matter.

The referenceCount of 1 reflects a deliberate and appropriate choice: three of the four research-packet sources (the Carbon Brief AC piece, the Guardian train video, and the Grist Pacific seabed mining story) have no substantive bearing on the wildfire story and were correctly excluded from citation. Single-source dependency is a valid concern in general, but here the one admitted source is a detailed wire-level report that itself draws on Canadian Press material and attributed statements from multiple named officials, and the manuscript's attribution chain makes the intermediary role of Grist visible throughout.

The `demand-lens-missing-h2` warning from the deterministic standards pass is noted. An 831-word breaking news piece built on a single strong news hook can credibly carry no section headers; the absence is not disqualifying. The SEO term appears in metadata fields (keywords, tags) in its raw machine form "thousands flee nations bear brunt canada" â€” that phrasing does not intrude into the prose, which is correct behavior.

The `gates_clear: false` flag in the frontmatter means the piece cannot be published without a further explicit gate clearance step, independent of this editorial review.

CLAIM AUDIT:
Location of Namaygoosisagagun (~125 miles north of Thunder Bay, road-inaccessible): supported. Community largely destroyed in under an hour on Wednesday: supported ("largely destroy its entire community in less than an hour"). Door-to-door warnings; more than two dozen fled by boat with minutes to spare: supported. Thirteen Nations under evacuation orders, 2,182 displaced: supported. 903 fires burning as of Friday, 750 out of control, 43 in prior 24 hours: supported. Seven First Nations evacuated in Ontario among 193 Ontario fires: supported. Named evacuated Nations (Whitesand First Nation, Kiashke Zaaging Anishinaabek, Animbiigoo Zaagi'igan Anishinaabek): supported. Debassige statement and title accurate: supported. No advance warning or evacuation support from provincial or national officials: supported. The Indian Act recognition gap and its consequences for federal emergency eligibility: supported. Meaghan Daniel's identity and the letter to Minister Gull-Masty: supported. Both quoted passages from Daniel's letter: accurate against source. Eric Head spokesperson statement: supported. Ontario Chiefs emergency meeting Wednesday: supported. Benedict quotes (both the "jurisdictional disputes" passage and the community solidarity remark): accurate against source. Assembly of First Nations emergency resolutions Thursday at the Ottawa assembly: supported. Anishinabek Nation statement and the quoted passage: accurate against source.

No invented causal claims were detected. No collapse of distinct Nations into a single analogy. No allegation strengthened beyond its stated force. The Grist byline is cited inline; the manuscript does not present sourced material as original Koios reporting.

REQUIRED REPAIRS:
The body contains five inline hyperlinks that read `https://grist.org/Indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/`. Each must be updated to the canonical admitted URL: `https://grist.org/indigenous/thousands-flee-as-first-nations-bear-the-brunt-of-canadas-wildfires/` (lowercase `i` in `indigenous`). The correction record in the frontmatter already logs this intent and specifies that the prose is unchanged; applying the fix to the body completes the correction. After that change, the `affected_surfaces` list in the correction record â€” article, feeds, sitemaps, json-ld, social-card, syndication â€” should be confirmed propagated per the correction surface policy. Once body text and all derivative surfaces use the canonical URL, a gate clearance pass can set `gates_clear: true` and promote the piece to publishable state.

The `demand-lens-missing-h2` warning does not require resolution before publication given the piece's format and length, but is available as an optional structural improvement.

PIPELINE EVIDENCE:
The production run is null, so there is no historical gate record to audit. The deterministic standards record shows `publishable: true` with `gates_clear: false` â€” a legal state in which the piece has passed factual and structural evaluation but has not been explicitly promoted through the final clearance step. Both standards findings carry severity `warning` and gate `standards-cleared`, meaning neither was treated as a hard block. The `attention-preserve-correction` finding recorded that a correction state existed and was noted; but the correction metadata and the body text diverged, with the body retaining the pre-correction URL form. The standards pass detected the correction's existence but did not verify that the body had been updated to match â€” that verification gap is the proximate pipeline failure.

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:
The standards review layer should add a verification step that compares each URL appearing in the body against the canonical URLs in the admitted source records, and treats a mismatch as a blocking error rather than a warning when a correction record has already been logged for that surface. The current behavior â€” recording a correction in metadata and flagging it as `standards-cleared` without confirming body propagation â€” creates exactly the inconsistency found here. A structural check of the form "if a correction's affected_surfaces includes 'article', confirm that the body text reflects the corrected state before marking the gate cleared" would prevent this class of defect from reaching editorial review.

